            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Precipitation ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 28.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              112.5199966430664,
              75.60729217529297,
              262.625,
              103.62728881835938
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "14.1",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              117.86051177978516,
              94.75901794433594,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Introduction ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              103.5199966430664,
              117.86051177978516,
              171.03500366210938,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Precipitation is any of the forms of water particles, whether liquid or solid, that fall from the atmosphere ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              138.60244750976562,
              542.3939819335938,
              149.5824432373047
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "and reach the ground. This chapter covers the necessary ingredients for formation, the growth process, and ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              151.20242309570312,
              542.7628173828125,
              162.1824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the types of precipitation. Some precipitation types include drizzle, rain, freezing rain, freezing drizzle, ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              163.86245727539062,
              542.5306396484375,
              174.8424530029297
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "snow, snow grains, ice crystals, ice pellets, hail, and small hail and/or snow pellets. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              176.52243041992188,
              441.0249938964844,
              187.50242614746094
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 166,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "14.2",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              79.6404800415039,
              94.75901794433594,
              92.66048431396484
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Necessary Ingredients for Formation ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              103.5199966430664,
              79.6404800415039,
              297.81500244140625,
              92.66048431396484
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Precipitation formation requires three ingredients: water vapor, sufficient lift to condense the water vapor ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              100.3624496459961,
              542.4378662109375,
              111.34245300292969
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "into clouds, and a growth process that allows cloud droplets to grow large and heavy enough to fall as ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              113.02242279052734,
              542.8049926757812,
              124.00242614746094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "precipitation. Significant precipitation usually requires clouds to be at least 4,000 ft thick. The heavier the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              125.7024154663086,
              542.70263671875,
              136.6824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "precipitation, the thicker the clouds are likely to be. When arriving or departing from an airport reporting ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              138.30245971679688,
              542.3170776367188,
              149.28245544433594
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "precipitation of light or greater intensity, expect clouds to be more than 4,000 ft thick. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              150.96243286132812,
              452.60498046875,
              161.9424285888672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "14.3",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              175.8404998779297,
              94.75901794433594,
              188.86048889160156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Growth Process ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              103.5199966430664,
              175.8404998779297,
              189.4550018310547,
              188.86048889160156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "All clouds contain water, but only some produce precipitation. This is because cloud droplets and/or ice ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              196.56246948242188,
              542.6974487304688,
              207.54246520996094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "crystals are too small and light to fall to the ground as precipitation. Because of their microscopic size, the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              209.22244262695312,
              542.4158325195312,
              220.2024383544922
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "rate at which cloud droplets fall is incredibly slow. An average cloud droplet falling from a cloud base ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              221.88241577148438,
              542.7129516601562,
              232.86241149902344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "at 3,300 ft (1,000 m) would require about 48 hours to reach the ground. It would never complete this ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              234.54244995117188,
              542.4863891601562,
              245.52244567871094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "journey, because it would evaporate within minutes after falling below the cloud base. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              247.20242309570312,
              454.1650085449219,
              258.1824035644531
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Two growth processes exist that allow cloud droplets (or ice crystals) to grow large enough to reach the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              265.8024597167969,
              542.8049926757812,
              276.7824401855469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "ground as precipitation before they evaporate (or sublimate). One process is called the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              278.4624328613281,
              542.7515869140625,
              289.4424133300781
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "collision-coalescence, or warm rain process (see Figure 14-1). In this process, collisions occur between ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              291.1224365234375,
              542.6637573242188,
              302.1024169921875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "cloud droplets of varying sizes and different fall speeds, sticking together or coalescing to form larger drops. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              303.7824401855469,
              542.6366577148438,
              314.7624206542969
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finally, the drops become too large to be suspended in the air, and they fall to the ground as rain. This is ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              316.44244384765625,
              542.3719482421875,
              327.42242431640625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "thought to be the primary growth process in warm, tropical air masses where the freezing level is very high. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              329.1024475097656,
              542.8049926757812,
              340.0824279785156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Most cloud droplets are too small and light to fall to the ground as precipitation. However, the larger cloud ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              347.70245361328125,
              542.4376831054688,
              358.68243408203125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "droplets fall more rapidly and are able to sweep up the smaller ones in their path and grow. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              360.3624572753906,
              474.92498779296875,
              371.3424377441406
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 167,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 168,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 169,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "can begin as rain and/or snow but becomes solely rain in the warm layer. The rain falls back into ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              73.42244720458984,
              542.5696411132812,
              84.40245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "below-freezing air, but since the depth is shallow, the rain does not have time to freeze into ice pellets ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              86.0824203491211,
              542.7635498046875,
              97.06242370605469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "(see Figure 14-4). The drops freeze on contact with the ground or exposed objects, such as aircraft. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              98.7424545288086,
              509.9649963378906,
              109.72245788574219
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p169_b11.png",
            "bbox": [
              198.0,
              126.44999694824219,
              413.989990234375,
              334.75
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 14-4. Freezing Rain Temperature Environment ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              346.735107421875,
              291.18499755859375,
              356.7550964355469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "14.4.4",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              371.50048828125,
              104.52401733398438,
              384.5205078125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rain ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              112.5199966430664,
              371.50048828125,
              140.31500244140625,
              384.5205078125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rain occurs when there is a deep layer of above freezing air based at the surface (see Figure 14-5). ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              392.2224426269531,
              508.8249816894531,
              403.2024230957031
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p169_b12.png",
            "bbox": [
              198.0,
              419.95001220703125,
              413.95001220703125,
              625.75
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 14-5. Rain Temperature Environment ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              637.2750854492188,
              253.68499755859375,
              647.2951049804688
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "14.4.5",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              662.04052734375,
              104.52401733398438,
              675.0604858398438
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hail ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              112.5199966430664,
              662.04052734375,
              138.15499877929688,
              675.0604858398438
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hail is precipitation in the form of balls or other irregular lumps of ice produced by thunderstorms. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              682.7824096679688,
              542.7452392578125,
              693.762451171875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thunderstorms that are characterized by strong updrafts, large Supercooled Liquid Water Content (SLWC), ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              695.4424438476562,
              542.8049926757812,
              706.4224853515625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "large cloud-drop sizes, and great vertical height, are favorable to hail formation. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              708.1024169921875,
              426.68499755859375,
              719.0824584960938
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 170,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hail forms when supercooled water droplets above the freezing level begin to freeze. Once a droplet has ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              73.42244720458984,
              542.361083984375,
              84.40245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "frozen, other droplets latch on and freeze to it, so the hailstone grows—sometimes into a huge ice ball. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              86.0824203491211,
              542.7999877929688,
              97.06242370605469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Large hail occurs with severe thunderstorms with strong updrafts that have built to great heights. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              98.68245697021484,
              542.4268798828125,
              109.66246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Eventually, the hailstones fall and may be encountered in clear air several miles from the thunderstorm. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              111.3424301147461,
              530.1849975585938,
              122.32243347167969
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "An individual unit of hail is called a hailstone. Hailstones can range in size from a pea (0.25-in diameter) ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              130.02243041992188,
              542.7892456054688,
              141.00242614746094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "to larger than a softball (4.5-in diameter). Hail competes with turbulence as the greatest thunderstorm hazard ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              142.68246459960938,
              542.6155395507812,
              153.66246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "to aircraft. Hailstones that are 0.75 in in diameter and larger can cause significant damage to aircraft and ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              155.34243774414062,
              542.5541381835938,
              166.3224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "make it difficult to control. A hailstone was collected at Vivian, SD, on July 23, 2010, that measured 8 in ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              168.00247192382812,
              542.6793823242188,
              178.9824676513672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "in diameter, 18.62 in in circumference, and weighed 1.93 lb (see Figure 14-6). ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              180.66244506835938,
              419.0050048828125,
              191.64244079589844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "As hailstones fall through air with temperatures above 0°C, they begin to melt, and precipitation may reach ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              199.26242065429688,
              542.7581787109375,
              210.24241638183594
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the ground as either hail or rain. Rain at the surface does not mean the absence of hail aloft. Pilots should ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              211.92245483398438,
              542.3609619140625,
              222.90245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "anticipate possible hail with any thunderstorm, especially beneath the anvil of a large cumulonimbus. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              224.58242797851562,
              520.4649658203125,
              235.5624237060547
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p170_b9.png",
            "bbox": [
              162.0,
              252.29000854492188,
              449.95001220703125,
              467.6400146484375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 14-6. Vivian, South Dakota, Record Hailstone ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              480.1351318359375,
              288.125,
              490.1551208496094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hail is most frequently found in the interior of continents within the mid-latitudes and generally confined ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              504.6224060058594,
              542.6797485351562,
              515.6024169921875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "to higher elevations within the tropics. In the United States, hail is most common across the Great Plains ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              517.2824096679688,
              542.63330078125,
              528.262451171875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "region east of the Rocky Mountains. Hail is more common aloft and at higher elevations, because the stones ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              529.9424438476562,
              542.78564453125,
              540.9224853515625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "begin to melt when they fall below the freezing level, and the smaller stones may melt into raindrops before ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              542.6024169921875,
              542.5256958007812,
              553.5824584960938
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "they reach the surface. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              555.2623901367188,
              173.7050018310547,
              566.242431640625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "When viewed from the air, it is evident that hail falls in paths known as hail swaths. They can range in size ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              573.9224243164062,
              542.5695190429688,
              584.9024658203125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "from a few acres to an area 10 mi wide and 100 mi long. Piles of hail in hail swaths have been deep enough ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              586.5223999023438,
              542.7177734375,
              597.50244140625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "to need a snowplow to remove them, and occasionally hail drifts have been reported. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              599.1824340820312,
              447.7449951171875,
              610.1624755859375
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
